[38;5;231m⏺[39m Using hyperpowers:using-hyperpowers — the ladder puts this at rung 1, so I'm flagging before editing.

  [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m holds [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m, and [38;5;153mserver.js:3[39m reads it as the session expiry. The change itself is
  one value (30 → 480), but session lifetime is security posture: a 16× longer window means a stolen session token, an
  unattended logged-in machine, or a hijacked session stays usable for 8 hours instead of 30 minutes, and
  revocation-by-expiry effectively stops being a control for a full working day. It also means sessions survive well
  past the point a user would assume they'd been logged out.

  That's a real consequence beyond the line I'd touch, so I want your explicit go-ahead rather than assuming it.

  Confirm and I'll set [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 480[39m — nothing else.

  Worth knowing before you answer: if the motivation is "users keep getting logged out mid-work," the usual fix is a
  sliding/idle timeout (reset on activity) with a shorter absolute cap, which keeps the security window small while
  removing the annoyance. Say the word and I'll do the straight bump anyway — it's your call, I just don't want to make
  it silently.

